WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.340
From Philadelphia bar owners to Pawnee government
officials, from New York ad men to Russian

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.860
spies, TV has only kept getting better over
the past decade. It's hard to narrow down

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:14.089
the best offerings, but we stand by these
as the best television shows airing during

00:00:14.089 --> 00:00:14.820
the 2010s.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:20.860
A show about five deadbeat lowlifes running
a bar in South Philadelphia doesn't exactly

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:25.699
sound like a comedic goldmine right off the
bat, but for better or for worse, It's Always

00:00:25.699 --> 00:00:30.810
Sunny in Philadelphia has defied all the odds
for years. After it got off to a relatively

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:37.140
inconspicuous and super-cheap start in 2005,
the show continued through the entire following

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.320
decade, truly becoming a comedy staple of
the 2010s. When it was renewed for its 14th

00:00:42.329 --> 00:00:46.450
season, it officially tied with The Adventures
of Ozzie and Harriet as the longest-running

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:51.520
live action sitcom of all time, which might
be the only way those two shows can be compared

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:52.060
to each other.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:56.470
"Take a look at yourself: you can't do backflips,
you don't know karate, you're white trash!"

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:59.000
"You're white trash, too, dude, look at those
shorts are white trash!

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.560
"Do not call the shorts white trash!"

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.790
So how exactly has Sunny stayed so fresh over
nearly 15 years? Thanks to brilliant scripts

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:11.530
by co-creators Rob McElhenney, Glenn Howerton,
and Charlie Day, who also play Mac, Dennis,

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:16.210
and Charlie, respectively, this totally bizarre
sitcom has kept things new and exciting even

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:21.479
as it enters the 2020s. From its innovative
approach to current issues to insane board

00:01:21.479 --> 00:01:26.980
games, ill-advised child beauty pageants,
the best plane trip of all time, "The Gang"

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:31.170
always keeps things interesting, and the series
even manages to hit its emotional beats without

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:36.080
seeming inauthentic. At the end of the show's
13th season, Mac came out to his father in

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.100
a gorgeously choreographed dance, proving
that Always Sunny will always be relevant,

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.360
no matter how long it's on the air.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.520
As the spiritual successor to The Office,
NBC's other workplace mockumentary Parks and

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:53.230
Recreation had huge shoes to fill. Ultimately,
it succeeded by creating an entirely different

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:58.720
show and relying on pure positivity rather
than cringe humor. The story of a small government

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.850
department in Pawnee, Indiana, led by the
intrepid and overly intense Leslie Knope in

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:08.920
an arguably career-defining role from Amy
Poehler, Parks &amp; Rec taught audiences that

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:12.820
they could achieve anything they wanted, even
if your life takes some unexpected turns.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:18.320
With a supporting cast rounded out by future
stars like Chris Pratt, Aziz Ansari, Aubrey

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:23.660
Plaza, and Adam Scott, alongside established
stars like Rob Lowe and Rashida Jones, the

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:28.500
show always survived on sheer optimism, even
as it repeatedly faced cancellation.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.300
Throughout its run, Parks &amp; Rec remained one
of the most cheerful and sunny shows on television,

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:39.190
letting its characters fail, learn, succeed,
and grow in realistic ways that rewarded its

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:43.640
fans as well as its talented cast. From its
many standout moments to its pitch-perfect

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:47.670
finale, it's a show that earned its place
in television history without having to stand

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.740
in the shadow of The Office at all.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:56.020
Zombies were all the rage during the 2000s,
and in 2010, AMC took advantage of the trend

00:02:56.030 --> 00:03:01.100
with the series premiere of The Walking Dead.
Based on a comic book series written by Robert

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:06.650
Kirkman, the show opens on Rick Grimes, played
by Andrew Lincoln in a role about as far from

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:11.840
Love Actually as he could get, as he wakes
up from a coma only to discover that the world

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:16.810
has fallen into ruin. After wandering through
a desolate Atlanta, he eventually finds other

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:21.280
survivors and must join forces with them to
fight against hordes of the undead.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:26.100
Throughout ten seasons, The Walking Dead has
gifted audiences with unforgettable heroes

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:31.580
like Rick, Michonne, and Glenn. It's also
given us formidable villains like Negan, played

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:36.860
with sadistic glee by Jeffrey Dean Morgan.
Together, they paint a bleak, and yet somehow

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:41.280
optimistic portrait of humanity as they face
down the most terrifying threat possible.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:45.600
Thanks to creative storytelling and impeccable
action, The Walking Dead has earned a place

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.870
as one of the most unforgettable shows of
the 2010s, and it seems poised to continue

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:50.780
for years to come.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.860
Ever since The Silence of the Lambs swept
the Academy Awards in 1992, audiences have

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:03.319
been fascinated with author Thomas Harris'
elegant cannibal, Dr. Hannibal Lecter. Showrunner

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.740
Bryan Fuller capitalized on the fictional
serial killer with his critically adored series

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:13.040
Hannibal, which ran for three seasons between
2013 and 2015. With Mads Mikkelsen as the

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.260
title killer and Hugh Dancy as an FBI investigator
Will Graham who finds himself caught up in

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:24.370
Hannibal's tangled web of mind games and dark
indulgences, the show was adored by its fans.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.280
"How does one politely refuse a dish in circumstances
such as these?"

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:31.360
"One doesn't."

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:37.200
Thanks to Fuller's trademark visual flourishes
and creative storytelling, Hannibal made its

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:41.580
mark on television as a whole. Despite the
fact that it was canceled by NBC due to low

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:46.660
ratings, it's still regarded as one of the
best shows of the modern era. Its legion of

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:51.630
"Fannibals" still pine for more, even though
the series finale brought the complex relationship

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:54.320
between its protagonists to a perfect crescendo.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:00.480
Spy thrillers have always been popular, and
in 2013, FX struck gold with The Americans.

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:06.140
This Cold War thriller focused on two Russian
spies, Elizabeth and Philip Jennings, played

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:11.350
by Keri Russell and Matthew Rhys, masquerading
as everyday Americans during one of the most

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:15.940
fraught and tense periods in American history.
Aside from hiding their true identities from

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:20.320
their friends and neighbors, they must also
keep their terrible secret from their children,

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:25.220
who were born as American citizens. It doesn't
help that their neighbor and good friend Stan

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.600
Beeman happens to be an FBI agent.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:32.780
Throughout six gripping seasons, The Americans
perfectly encapsulated the turmoil of the

00:05:32.781 --> 00:05:37.640
Cold War while telling the personal story
of the Jennings, their undercover colleagues,

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:38.240
and their friends.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.380
"She's trained for this. So am I."

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.260
"We'll die before we'll talk."

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:45.100
"And we know how to do that."

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:50.170
The show features powerhouse performances
by Russell and Rhys, whose onscreen chemistry

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:55.400
led to the two of them striking up a romance
on set and having a child together in 2016.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:00.831
It also boasts incisive writing and direction,
especially in its final season, landing it

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.440
firmly on the list of the best shows of the
2010s.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:09.200
In the opening moments of The Good Place,
the audience meets Eleanor Shellstrop, played

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:14.120
by Kristen Bell. She has just been given some
good news and some bad news by Michael, played

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:19.330
by Ted Danson: she recently died, but on the
upside, she made it into the afterlife called

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:23.770
"The Good Place." As she spends more time
in paradise, Eleanor starts to realize that

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:28.830
something just isn't right, and eventually,
she confesses to her "soulmate" Chidi that

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:32.340
there's been some sort of mistake, and she
actually doesn't belong here.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:35.520
"I'm not supposed to be here."

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:39.860
"Wait, what?"

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:44.940
That's just the first twist of The Good Place's
debut season, and anyone who sits down and

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:50.100
watches this innovative show will be rewarded
through every unbelievable twist and turn

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:54.630
it takes. Alongside TV veterans like Bell
and Danson, the cast is mostly made up of

00:06:54.630 --> 00:07:00.090
fresh faces. including William Jackson Harper
costars as the critically indecisive Chidi.

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:05.340
Manny Jacinto, plays "Florida dirtbag" Jason,
who himself sometimes masquerades as a silent

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:10.090
Buddhist monk. In her breakout role, D'arcy
Carden plays Janet, and all-knowing sort of

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:15.510
human Alexa device, and British television
presenter Jameela Jamil, plays the oblivious,

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:19.930
name-dropping socialite Tahani. Though it
might feel like you're in the Bad Place when

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:23.590
you hear that The Good Place is calling it
quits with the end of its fourth season in

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:27.920
2020, Schur and his team certainly know what
they're doing. With sharp writing and what

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:32.770
seems like a new twist every episode, they've
gifted audiences with one of the most inventive

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:33.760
sitcoms in recent memory.

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:39.760
One of the defining dramatic shows of the
entire 21st century thus far has been AMC's

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:46.070
Mad Men, which starred Jon Hamm as the enigmatic
and mysterious Don Draper, a suave, smooth-talking

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:50.740
New York City ad man with secrets to hide.
Led by a showrunner who was a veteran of The

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:56.160
Sopranos, the rest of Mad Men's cast was rounded
out by talented performers like Elisabeth

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:01.991
Moss, January Jones, John Slattery, Christina
Hendricks, and more. The end result was a

00:08:01.991 --> 00:08:07.419
group of extraordinary actors playing characters
who were each given their own complex emotional

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:07.920
journeys.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:13.310
Ultimately, the series covered all of the
1960s, recreating moments like the moon landing,

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:18.990
the assassination of JFK, and more, all set
against Don Draper's extraordinarily turbulent

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:24.090
life. By the end of the show, Don's life is
in tatters. After betraying his second wife

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:29.050
Megan, he finds himself alone in California
and seemingly contemplating ending it all,

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:34.700
but just as the series ends, he takes a turn
thanks to a tranquil seaside resort. In the

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:38.900
story's final moments, we see Don sitting
alone on a cliffside, meditating quietly,

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:45.400
and then breaking into a mysterious smile...followed
by one of Coca-Cola's most famous real-world

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:50.500
ads, insinuating that Don himself came up
with it. Finding capitalist inspiration during

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:55.241
meditation would be a classic Don move, and
though the ending is largely ambiguous, it's

00:08:55.241 --> 00:08:59.880
a perfect finish for Mad Men's overall journey,
cementing it as a classic of the decade.

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:06.580
When it premiered in 2011, Game of Thrones
probably seemed like an unlikely success for

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:12.240
HBO. An adaptation of George R.R. Martin's
sprawling fantasy series A Song of Ice and

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:17.260
Fire, the show, named after the first book
in the series, stuck faithfully to Martin's

00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:21.890
narrative. Set in the fictional region of
Westeros, Thrones spent most of the decade

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:26.760
telling the story of its most powerful players,
all of whom spend the entire series fighting

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:32.320
for the Iron Throne. From the Dragon Queen,
Daenerys Targaryen to the power-hungry Cersei

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:37.560
Lannister to the conflicted but noble Jon
Snow, the Iron Throne was up for grabs to

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:42.380
any number of qualified candidates, and for
years, diehard fans tuned in to see who would

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:43.880
win the game in the end.

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:49.880
"When people ask you what happened here, tell
them the north remembers."

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:54.720
As it turned out, Game of Thrones ended not
with a bang, but with a whimper. Thanks to

00:09:54.730 --> 00:10:00.681
a poorly plotted final season and an unsatisfying
inheritor of the throne, fans flocked to the

00:10:00.681 --> 00:10:05.800
internet to express their extreme displeasure.
Still, a disappointing final season doesn't

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:10.620
undo Thrones' legacy. As one of the last great
examples of a show that everyone had to make

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:15.070
time for each week, Thrones changed TV for
the better, and it remains one of the most

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:18.040
impressive accomplishments on television in
recent memory.

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:23.000
There have been plenty of political television
shows throughout the years, but few of them

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:29.540
have been as cruel, incisive, and hilarious
as Veep. The series was led by Julia Louis-Dreyfus

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:34.240
as the titular Vice President, Selina Meyer,
whose political dreams were crushed at the

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:39.280
beginning of the series as she was relegated
to the second-place spot alongside an unseen

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:44.430
president. Through it all, Veep never shied
away from showing the dark underbelly of Washington

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:48.580
D.C., from underhanded deals to vicious insults
to destructive policies.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:50.940
"They're calling you the 'No B.S. V.P.'."

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:56.600
"Damn right they are! I mean, I lied and everything,
but it sounded true, at least."

00:10:57.140 --> 00:11:02.940
Selina is aided and abetted by her equally
corrupt team, including "bag man" and eternally

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:09.160
loyal right hand man Gary, Chief of Staff
Amy, communications expert Dan, speechwriter

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:15.220
Mike, and arrogant White House liaison Jonah.
Unfortunately, they can't stop her constant

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:20.390
gaffes, as she makes political fumbles over
and over again. Despite the odds, she ends

00:11:20.390 --> 00:11:25.240
up serving as president of the United States
multiple times. Thanks to a tour-de-force

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:29.940
performance by Louis-Dreyfus, some of the
sharpest writing on television, and a pitch-perfect

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:34.870
supporting cast, Veep hit all the right notes,
even while it made you concerned about the

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:36.200
state of American politics.

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:42.600
Animated series for adults filled the airwaves
during the first two decades of the 21st century,

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:48.210
and though there are plenty of excellent examples,
a standout series was Netflix's BoJack Horseman,

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:53.029
created by Raphael Bob-Waksberg. Set in a
surreal world populated with normal humans

00:11:53.029 --> 00:11:58.360
as well as anthropomorphic talking animals
who act exactly like humans, the show followed

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:04.010
its title character, voiced by Will Arnett.
Bojack is a washed-up sitcom star who is constantly

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:08.600
trying to regain celebrity, having starred
in the once-popular series Horsin' Around.

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:13.480
As he plans his return to the public eye,
BoJack works with Diane Nguyen, played by

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:18.640
Community and GLOW star Alison Brie, a ghostwriter
he hires to help write his autobiography.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:23.120
Through it all, he haggles with his cat agent
Princess Carolyn and butts heads with his

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:25.110
sitcom nemesis, Mr. Peanutbutter.

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:29.060
"I have nothing to show for the life that
I've lived, and I have nobody in my life who's

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:30.510
better off for having known me."

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:31.280
"That isn't true."

00:12:31.529 --> 00:12:32.280
"Isn't it though?"

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:37.740
Despite the seemingly ridiculous premise,
Bob-Waksburg and his impeccable voice cast

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:42.490
use their platform to address issues like
clinical depression and marital strife, proving

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:46.240
that there's so much more to BoJack Horseman
than initially meets the eye.

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:52.360
Phoebe Waller-Bridge's one-woman show turned
television sensation Fleabag only ran for

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:57.850
two seasons, but it made an undeniably explosive
impact during that short amount of time. The

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:02.460
first season of this bleak half-hour comedy
stars Waller-Bridge herself as "Fleabag,"

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:07.080
a single, depressed, and uninhibited London
woman with little direction in her life. The

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:11.240
main character struggles with her best friend's
untimely death while she tries to maintain

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:15.830
relationships with her difficult family and
series of boyfriends. Throughout the show,

00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:19.800
she constantly breaks the fourth wall, treating
the viewer like a best friend from whom she

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:25.570
hides absolutely nothing. Waller-Bridge was
originally reluctant about a second season,

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:30.240
but audiences are lucky she went for it; in
2019, the showrunner released a thoughtful,

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:34.899
emotional, and beautifully crafted second
season, in which Fleabag forms a confusing

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:37.980
relationship with a priest, played by Andrew
Scott.

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:41.000
"This is a love story."

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:46.380
Between its engaging storytelling, its talented
roster of series regulars and guest stars,

00:13:46.399 --> 00:13:50.899
which includes everyone from Olivia Colman
to Kristin Scott Thomas, and Waller-Bridge's

00:13:50.899 --> 00:13:55.589
beautifully unique voice, Fleabag is one of
the best-reviewed shows to hit the airwaves

00:13:55.589 --> 00:14:00.970
in years. At the 2019 Emmys, it dominated
the conversation, even taking the top prize

00:14:00.970 --> 00:14:05.740
from the final season of Veep. Waller-Bridge
says she's definitely done with the character

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:10.050
now, and while that might be disappointing
for some fans, it also means that Fleabag

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:13.640
can remain impeccable, unchanged, and endlessly
rewatchable.

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:19.620
Ever since his time on Community, Donald Glover
has been a talent to watch. He proved himself

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:25.230
on the small screen once again in 2016 with
FX's Atlanta, in which he stars as well as

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:30.020
writes, directs, and produces. Glover plays
Earn, who's trying to make his way in Atlanta

00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:34.560
when he realizes that his talented cousin
Alfred, who raps as "Paper Boi," might have

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:38.730
what it takes to make it. If Paper Boi can
make it with Earn's help, then Earn might

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:41.380
be able to reunite with his daughter and estranged
girlfriend.

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:45.780
Over just two seasons, Atlanta has become
one of the most talked-about shows of the

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:50.671
decade, earning universal praise for Glover's
performance and behind-the-scenes work. In

00:14:50.671 --> 00:14:56.140
the show's second season, subtitled "Robbin'
Season," Glover took even more risks, and

00:14:56.140 --> 00:15:00.820
they've paid off handsomely with things like
the creepy, riveting, and unforgettable "Teddy

00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:01.820
Perkins" episode.

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:06.560
"You're actually the first person to be in
here. Feel free to Twitter or Blogspot any

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:07.060
of it."

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:10.440
"Uhhh you know what? Actually, I'm kind of
in a rush."

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:15.480
Glover even became the first African American
artist to win an Emmy for directing a comedy

00:15:15.490 --> 00:15:20.480
series, so as long as Glover wants to stay
in Atlanta, fans and critics alike will definitely

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:24.420
tune in, and anyone who's watched the show
just can't wait to see what he does next.

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:30.080
Netflix has produced plenty of hugely successful
original shows, but few have been as heavily

00:15:30.089 --> 00:15:34.019
discussed and stirred up as much excitement
as Stranger Things, a show from the Duffer

00:15:34.019 --> 00:15:38.800
Brothers that first hit the streaming service
in 2016. Set in the seemingly sleepy town

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:43.329
of Hawkins, Indiana, Stranger Things introduced
viewers to the frightening dimension of "the

00:15:43.329 --> 00:15:48.660
Upside Down" and monsters like the Demogorgon.
Its story began with the sudden disappearance

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:53.120
of young Will Byers and the efforts by his
friends and family to find him. As they delve

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:58.170
deeper into the unexpected and sinister world
that lies beneath Hawkins, Will's friend Mike

00:15:58.170 --> 00:16:02.930
befriends a mysterious girl who goes by Eleven,
and who turns out to have surprising special

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:03.420
powers.

00:16:04.010 --> 00:16:08.220
Stranger Things has earned praise and adoration
for its clever plotting, hearty sense of humor,

00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:13.980
and outstanding young cast, all of whom have
skyrocketed to fame since the show debuted.

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:19.150
Alongside this new generation of actors, veterans
like Winona Ryder, Sean Astin, and David Harbour

00:16:19.150 --> 00:16:23.910
deliver excellent performances as well, making
sure that the adults of Stranger Things are

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:29.089
just as compelling as the kids. After dominating
the second half of the decade, Stranger Things

00:16:29.089 --> 00:16:33.800
shows no signs of slowing down, especially
after a cliffhanger capped off its third season

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:34.800
in 2019.

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:40.980
There are too few shows that chronicle the
pure joys of female friendship, but in 2014,

00:16:40.990 --> 00:16:45.750
creators and showrunners Abbi Jacobson and
Ilana Glazer gifted the world with Broad City.

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:50.579
This laugh-out-loud comedy is an unflinching
exploration of the highs and lows of the particularly

00:16:50.579 --> 00:16:56.310
intense relationship between its stars' fictional
counterparts, also named Abbi and Ilana. The

00:16:56.310 --> 00:17:00.690
two comedians met while studying at New York
City's Upright Citizens Brigade, and after

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:05.339
they produced Broad City as a web series,
UCB founder Amy Poehler took notice. Before

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:10.429
long, the comedy star was serving as executive
producer alongside Jacobson and Glazer when

00:17:10.429 --> 00:17:13.909
the series moved to Comedy Central to begin
its official run.

00:17:13.909 --> 00:17:18.939
From romantic mishaps to counterfeit bags
to outlandish outfits, Broad City covered

00:17:18.939 --> 00:17:24.029
every possible part of Abbi and Ilana's lives,
relationships, and enduring friendship, leaving

00:17:24.029 --> 00:17:29.360
no stone unturned, no matter how grotesque.
Broad City always pushed the limits, but it

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:34.530
also provided honest, emotional moments, giving
it a dynamic unlike any other show that aired

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:35.700
in the past ten years.

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:41.060
Adapting a beloved classic film into a television
show can definitely be a tricky endeavor and

00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:46.540
lead to middling results, but thanks to excellent
scripts and a revolving door of talented actors,

00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:52.320
showrunner Noah Hawley succeeded with 2014's
Fargo. The show is adapted from the award-winning

00:17:52.330 --> 00:17:57.710
1996 Coen Brothers film of the same name,
and the Academy Award-winning duo also lent

00:17:57.710 --> 00:18:02.000
their names to the project as executive producers,
giving it even more prestige.

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:07.320
An anthology series that airs on FX, Fargo
features a different cast and focuses on a

00:18:07.320 --> 00:18:11.791
different story for each season, but it cleverly
includes some overlap between the stories

00:18:11.791 --> 00:18:17.270
to make sure each one is connected. Of course,
every season features plenty of sly Coen references

00:18:17.270 --> 00:18:22.639
for their diehard fans. With a cadre of incredible
actors like Ewan McGregor, Jean Smart, Billy

00:18:22.639 --> 00:18:28.549
Bob Thornton, Martin Freeman, and more, audiences
will definitely remain riveted by Fargo for

00:18:28.549 --> 00:18:30.880
however long Hawley keeps the show going.

